# fiki — Intent Tree (this.i)
#
# The source of truth for why this repo is built the way it is. Code and docs/ are derived from
# it; every consequential decision lands here first, in its own commit, before the code it
# justifies. See bakobo/dev/methodology.md for the rules and AGENTS.md for this repo's stanza.
#
# Nodes are written with the `i` CLI (`i add <type> "<name>" --parent <nid> --why "..."`), which
# mints the ids. Never hand-type an id.
#
# fiki was carved out of bakobo/heti; the decision to split lives in heti's tree at @3qewrm0p,
# with @m4x7rc above it as the packaging decision it revisits. Nodes here cite those by id.

fiki proves which key sent an HTTP request, with no KERI stack on either end = goal:
  nid: 07wstqk7
  why: >-
    fiki lets a party with no KERI stack prove which key sent an HTTP request, and lets a Bakobo service check that proof. It is standard RFC 9421 with one lens: a bare Ed25519 public key is rendered as a non-transferable AID (CESR Ed25519N, a 44-character "B..." string), so the identifier IS the verifying key and a verifier needs no key event log, no directory lookup, and no network call to recover it. A client registers its AID once and signs from then on; there is nothing to rotate and nothing to fetch. Chose to carve this out of bakobo/heti (heti @m4x7rc, @3qewrm0p) rather than tell such a party to install heti, because heti reaches KERI through keripy and keripy brings 24 direct dependencies including lmdb, pysodium, falcon, and hio -- an unreasonable price for an ESB client, a cron job, or a container that only needs to say who it is. fiki depends on cryptography and http_sfv and on nothing else, so it must never import keripy and must never import bakobo-errors (@8zw78n0v); heti maps fiki's exceptions onto its own codes at the boundary. Chose the standards-vanilla dialect over the KERI flavor keria and signify-ts speak, because that flavor's signature base diverges from RFC 9421 in three ways and porting it to another language means reimplementing those divergences forever, whereas vanilla means "your language's RFC 9421 library plus a 40-line AID codec." Rejected making this a subpackage of heti behind an import boundary, which is what heti @m4x7rc chose in the first place and explicitly named its own falsifier for: a code boundary buys no smaller dependency tree, and the footprint is the entire point here. Rejected a repo per language, because the conformance vectors would fork. Tradeoff: fiki cannot talk to keria or to anything else speaking the KERI flavor, and a Bakobo service that wants to accept both dialects has to run both libraries.
  children:
    The default covered set closes the query, host, and body gaps, and fails closed = decision:
      nid: 2hwvpm42
      why: >-
        fiki's default covered set is @method, @authority, @path, @query, and created, plus a Content-Digest whenever the request carries a body. Signing a request that has a body and no Content-Digest RAISES rather than proceeding, and on the verify side fiki recomputes the digest over the received body and rejects a mismatch instead of trusting the header because the signature covers it. Chose this over heti's inherited set (Signify-Resource, @method, @path, Signify-Timestamp) because that set covers neither the query string, the host, nor the body: RFC 9421 has @path stop at the question mark, so a signed GET /things?limit=1 is indistinguishable from ?limit=1000000, and a POST body can be swapped wholesale. That is structural in the KERI dialect -- keripy's ending.siginput has no else branch for other derived components, which heti @h4s6tz books as its standing tradeoff and which is the subject of WebOfTrust/keripy#1669 -- and fiki is not under that ceiling, so it does not inherit the hole. Chose to refuse an unsigned body rather than warn, because a signing-side warning is read by nobody and a verifier cannot discover after the fact that the body was never covered; fail closed is the org's eighth principle. Chose to recompute the digest rather than verify the signature alone, for the same reason: a covered Content-Digest attests to a body nobody hashed, which is a guarantee about a header rather than about the request. Rejected covering @target-uri instead of the @authority/@path/@query trio: it binds the scheme, which a client behind a TLS-terminating proxy cannot reproduce. Rejected a caller opt-out on digest recomputation for streaming verifiers, because an opt-out is where fail-open returns; a streaming verifier is a real case and it can have a decision of its own later. Tradeoff: verification needs the whole body in memory, so fiki cannot verify a streamed request, and a fiki-signed request is not verifiable by heti's KERI path at all because the covered sets do not overlap -- the dialect is a deployment-time choice rather than a fallback chain.
    fiki raises its own exceptions and depends on no Bakobo error package = decision:
      nid: 8zw78n0v
      why: >-
        fiki raises its own exceptions and depends on no Bakobo error package. A small closed set of classes descends from one base, so a consumer can catch the base or discriminate by type, and heti maps each class onto its existing e.input.* and e.proof.* codes at the boundary with a test that walks the base class's subclasses and fails if any class has no mapping. Chose fiki-owned exceptions over depending on bakobo-errors because a dependency that travels into every language port is a dependency every port has to reimplement, and the point of the split is a library a cron job can install; bakobo-errors would also export Bakobo's sorter/descriptor/disposition grammar and its house voice into Go and JS, where none of it is wanted. Chose class-based discrimination over an error-code string attribute because the totality test then has a mechanical subject -- __subclasses__ -- rather than a registry someone must remember to update. Rejected heti catching a single fiki base exception and raising one generic code, which would collapse the taxonomy heti @x2r7mv built deliberately. Tradeoff: two vocabularies for the same failures, and the plain-sentence titles Bakobo's error standard requires live only on heti's side, so a caller using fiki directly gets a worse message than a caller reaching it through heti.
    Two conformance oracles — RFC 9421's own vectors, plus one committed Bakobo vector set = decision:
      nid: 5gf6r08f
      why: >-
        fiki's conformance rests on two oracles, and the repo is laid out so they cannot fork. RFC 9421's own Appendix B vectors come first, and they are usable on the SIGNING side rather than only for verification, because Appendix B.1.4 publishes the Ed25519 private key (PEM, and as a JWK with d = n4Ni-HpISpVObnQMW0wOhCKROaIKqKtW_2ZYb2p9KcU) and Ed25519 is deterministic with created pinned at 1618884473 -- so signing B.2.6's request reproduces the RFC's own Signature header byte for byte. Chose that as the first test written and the first test run red, because no Bakobo party authored it and it fails for a reason we cannot argue with. Verified 2026-09-03 against the RFC text itself rather than a summary: a WebFetch of the same appendix returned a fabricated public key and a fabricated B.2.6 component list, both plausible and both wrong, so no byte-exact value in this repo is ever taken from a summarizing tool. The second oracle is a committed vectors/ set at the repo root for what the RFC cannot cover, which is more than it sounds: B.2.6 exercises neither @query nor content-digest, and its own test request carries a query string it does not sign, so the two protections @2hwvpm42 adds beyond heti have NO RFC oracle and the Bakobo vectors are the only thing standing behind them. The vectors also carry the AID lens (a 32-byte Ed25519 key to a B-prefix non-transferable AID and back) and the refuse-a-body-without-Content-Digest rule. The polyglot layout -- py/, later go/ and js/, over one top-level vectors/ -- exists for exactly this reason rather than for tidiness: a repo per language would give each port its own copy of the vectors to age independently. Rejected generating the Bakobo vectors from keripy, which would reintroduce the dependency this repo exists to remove and would pin fiki to a reference that is not the standard; the AID codec is instead cross-checked against keripy inside HETI's suite, where keripy is present anyway, and byte-equality there is what keeps fiki's key type and heti's EphemeralKey interchangeable over one seed. Rejected deferring the vectors until a second language actually exists, because they are the only thing that makes a port checkable and a hand-port without them is a fork with a delay on it. Tradeoff: py/ carries a directory level nothing needs today, and the vectors must be regenerated deliberately when the default covered set changes, with nothing gating that -- so each vector file names the covered set it was generated under rather than relying on anyone to remember.
      children:
        'The JavaScript port: browser-first, zero dependencies, async, non-extractable keys by default = decision':
          nid: 2q9gv70t
          why: >-
            The JavaScript port targets browsers first and runs unchanged on Node, with ZERO runtime dependencies. Ed25519, SHA-256 and randomness all come from WebCrypto, which every current browser and Node 20+ provide, so the port needs no crypto library; the only other thing it would have imported is an RFC 8941 structured-fields parser, and it hand-rolls that instead. Chose hand-rolling over depending on `structured-headers` because fiki's subset of RFC 8941 is small and CLOSED -- a dictionary of inner lists with parameters, plus byte sequences -- because a browser library with no dependencies ships as one file with no supply chain to audit, and because @5gf6r08f's vectors already pin the exact serialized @signature-params line and the unparsable-header refusals, so a hand-rolled parser has a real oracle rather than a reviewer's confidence. Rejected the reverse for the usual reason a parser is the wrong thing to write yourself: that argument holds where the grammar is open-ended, and this one is 200 lines whose entire output surface is pinned by committed bytes. THE API IS ASYNC AND THAT IS FORCED, not chosen: WebCrypto's sign, verify, digest and importKey all return promises, so signRequest and verifyRequest are async where Python's are not, and that asymmetry is the one place a reader who knows one port will be surprised by the other. Everything else keeps the Python names in camelCase -- signRequest, verifyRequest, signatureBase, verifyingKey, Key.fromSeed, key.aid -- so the two ports read as one library. KEYS ARE NON-EXTRACTABLE BY DEFAULT, with generate({extractable: true}) as the opt-in: a browser key that JavaScript cannot read cannot be exfiltrated by an XSS bug, which is the dominant threat for a browser-held signing key, and the cost is that an identity is bound to one browser profile and a new device registers a new AID. Chose the safe default with an explicit escape hatch over matching Python's always-extractable Key, because the two runtimes have genuinely different threat models and making the browser inherit the server's would be pretending otherwise; a caller who needs a portable identity says so, and `key.seed` throws rather than returning nothing when the key cannot produce one. Note what makes fromSeed possible at all: WebCrypto refuses a raw 32-byte private key but accepts PKCS#8, and a PKCS#8 Ed25519 private key is a fixed 16-byte DER prefix followed by the seed, so the conversion is a concatenation rather than an ASN.1 encoder. Verified 2026-09-04 before this was written: Node 24's WebCrypto reproduces the default-covered-with-query vector's signature and the AID lens byte for byte. Tests run under node:test with no framework, at the same 100% branch gate the Python port holds, enforced by --test-coverage-branches=100. Tradeoff: two implementations of one wire format now exist and can drift, which the vectors bound but do not eliminate -- they catch what they cover -- and a port with no named consumer is a maintenance obligation taken on before anyone has asked for it, accepted deliberately because JavaScript is the broadest reach and the vectors were the precondition.
        The Go, Java, and Rust ports follow the JavaScript one, differing only where their standard libraries force it = decision:
          nid: 2tt6fmc0
          why: >-
            The Go, Java, and Rust ports follow the JavaScript one (@2q9gv70t) and share its shape: the same names in each language's casing, the same error taxonomy by CLASS NAME because refusals.json pins those strings, and the same required max_age. Each is checked against @5gf6r08f's vectors and against nothing else that could disagree with them. What differs between the three is only what their standard libraries force, and those differences are the reason to write this node rather than repeat @2q9gv70t. DEPENDENCIES ARE NOT UNIFORM AND CANNOT BE. Go's standard library carries ed25519 and sha256, so the Go port has zero dependencies like the JS one. Java's has had Ed25519 since JDK 15, so the Java port has zero RUNTIME dependencies, though it needs a build tool and a JSON reader to run the vectors, both confined to test scope. Rust's standard library has no cryptography at all, so the Rust port MUST take a dependency; it takes ed25519-dalek, and that asymmetry is recorded here rather than discovered by a reader who expected the zero-dependency claim to be universal. Chose to keep the RFC 8941 subset hand-rolled in every port, on @2q9gv70t's reasoning: the subset is small and closed, the vectors pin its entire output surface byte for byte, and a supply-chain dependency per language is a wider attack surface than the same 200 lines written five times where each one is checked against the same bytes. Rejected picking a structured-fields crate or module per language, which would have been less code and would have made five implementations depend on five maintainers none of whom know fiki exists. Chose to write all three now rather than on demand, which is Daniel's call recorded here rather than mine: the standing argument against it is that a port with no consumer is a maintenance obligation taken on before anyone asked, and the vectors bound the drift without eliminating it since they catch only what they cover. Tradeoff: five implementations of one wire format, and any change to the covered-set default or the error taxonomy is now a five-repo edit rather than a one-line one, which is the cost that buys reach.
        Each port versions independently, and every port declares the vectors format it satisfies = decision:
          nid: 4fhrre0m
          why: >-
            Each port versions independently, and every port additionally declares the VECTORS FORMAT it satisfies. Two artifacts interoperate when their declared vectors format matches, regardless of their own version numbers -- so fiki-py 0.5.0 and fiki-go 0.5.3 are interchangeable if both say vectors format 1, and a Java 0.6.0 that says format 2 is visibly not. Chose this over a single lockstep version across all five, which is simpler to reason about and was the other real option: lockstep would force an empty release of four artifacts every time one of them fixed a typo in a docstring, and worse, it would make the version number LOOK like an interoperability statement while actually being a release-coordination artifact -- a reader comparing 0.5.0 to 0.5.1 could not tell whether the wire behaviour had moved. Splitting the two facts apart says exactly one thing with each number. THE VECTORS FORMAT IS A MONOTONIC INTEGER, not a semantic version, because a conformance contract has no meaningful minor: an implementation either satisfies the vectors or it does not, and even ADDING a case is breaking for an implementation that already shipped, since it will now fail something it never implemented. It starts at 1 and increments whenever the behaviour the vectors require changes. It is declared in each vector file's header and exported as a constant by every port, and each port's vector driver asserts the two agree -- so a port running vectors from a newer format fails loudly rather than passing a subset and reporting conformance it does not have. All five ports start at 0.5.0 rather than 0.1.0 because the surface is further along than a first release implies: five implementations, 37 shared cases, and a wire contract that has already survived a consumer. Rejected 1.0.0, which would assert a stability nobody has asked for yet -- @3qewrm0p says the API is not stable, and heti still pins by commit. Tradeoff: five version numbers to move rather than one, and the discipline of remembering that a change to the covered-set default or the error taxonomy is a vectors-format bump and not merely a release.
    The key travels inline in keyid, as raw bytes rather than as the AID = decision:
      nid: 7xrx5evg
      why: >-
        The signer's key travels in RFC 9421's own keyid parameter, as the RAW 32-byte Ed25519 public key in base64url with no padding -- the RFC 8037 JWK "x" form, 43 characters -- and NOT as the 44-character B-prefix AID. So a request carries its own verifying key inline and a verifier resolves nothing; the AID remains what a human registers and reads, and fiki derives it from the keyid on the verify side rather than transmitting it. This is not a fresh choice: heti's vanilla dialect already fixes it at src/heti/ephemeral/vanilla.py:_resolve_key, whose @c3m5wp chose the inline raw-key form over a directory lookup because both of its recovery paths must stay pure, and cited draft-hardt-httpbis-signature-key and Cloudflare's web-bot-auth as the external convention. fiki emitting the AID there instead would mean heti could not verify a fiki-signed request at all, which would defeat the interop the split exists to preserve, so the decision is inherited rather than reopened. Chose one form rather than accepting both, because a keyid a verifier has to sniff is an interop ambiguity that shows up as a signature failure at somebody else's site. Chose to keep sign_request's keyid NON-overridable, unlike the lower-level signature_base, so that "the keyid is the key" is a property of every fiki-signed request rather than a default: the RFC's own Appendix B.2.6 vector uses a symbolic keyid ("test-key-ed25519") and is reproduced through signature_base for exactly that reason. Rejected carrying the AID in a separate header the way the KERI dialect carries Signify-Resource, which would put the identity outside RFC 9421's own vocabulary and give an off-the-shelf verifier one more thing it does not implement. Tradeoff: a verifier holding a preregistered AID must convert it to raw bytes to compare against keyid, which is two lines but is a conversion that would not exist if the AID travelled directly; and a fiki-signed request is pseudonymous by construction, since an inline key attests to itself and to nothing else.
    'fiki reads a clock: expires is always enforced, max_age is a required decision = decision':
      nid: 67shl6c5
      why: >-
        fiki reads a clock, and verify_request enforces freshness. Two conditions, decided separately because they have different owners. EXPIRES IS ALWAYS ENFORCED and takes no parameter: the signer put it there, it is bound into the signature base so it cannot be tampered with, and RFC 9421 section 3.2.1 says a verifier may reject a signature ahead of its expiration but never that it may ignore one -- a library that accepts an expires it does not honor is selling the caller a guarantee it has not bought, which is the defect ~3qdj named. MAX AGE IS A REQUIRED KEYWORD with no default, so a caller states a tolerance in seconds or states None, and either way the decision is written at the call site. Chose a required argument over a default because both defaults are wrong: a value guesses at somebody else's clock skew and replay window, and None reproduces the silent skip this whole change exists to remove -- the same fail-closed reasoning @2hwvpm42 used for the body. Section 3.2.1 is the authority for both halves: it lists maximum-age enforcement and expiry rejection as APPLICATION requirements rather than baseline ones, and then says that when an application defines a requirement it MUST enforce it during verification. So the RFC does not tell fiki what the policy is; it tells fiki that a declared policy must bite. A skew allowance defaults to 5 seconds and is adjustable, because two hosts disagreeing by a second is ordinary and a verifier that treats that as an attack is unusable. `now` is injectable, which is what keeps @5gf6r08f's vectors byte-exact: a conformance vector cannot pin a check against a moving clock, and a port would otherwise have to fake time to run them. fiki reads the clock ONLY when it needs to -- when expires is present, or when max_age is not None -- so a request that declares no freshness at all still verifies deterministically. Rejected enforcing a default max_age: it would break every existing vector, all of which are signed at a fixed created in the past, and breaking them is the loud symptom of a guess nobody made deliberately. Rejected leaving expires to the same required-parameter treatment as max_age: the two are not symmetric, since expires is the SIGNER's declaration and max_age is the VERIFIER's, and requiring a verifier to opt in to honoring a signer's own expiry inverts who is asserting what. Tradeoff: verify_request is no longer a pure function, so fiki's purity claim now has an exception with a name; heti's vanilla dialect must pass max_age through and passes None explicitly, which is heti's standing position under @p3w5kq rather than an oversight, and a heti caller who wants freshness now has somewhere for that to go.
